digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper aims to develop and evaluate high-throughput whole-brain c-Fos imaging in mice, combined with explainable machine-learning classification, as a preclinical screening strategy for differentiating psychedelic and related psychoactive compounds and prioritizing candidates for therapeutic development.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A preclinical screening platform is scientifically motivated when there is a need for complementary scalable screening, a measurable brain-wide biological readout, and interpretable classification/validation across multiple conditions."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Because existing screening approaches rely on mechanistic assumptions or weakly predictive behavioral assays, developing a complementary screening modality is warranted."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) A complementary and scalable screening method may help accelerate preclinical drug discovery for novel psychoactive or psychedelic-inspired therapeutic candidates."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Immediate early genes are activated in response to increased firing activity."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) Classic psychedelic administration induces increased expression of c-Fos, an immediate early gene."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) Advances in light-sheet fluorescence microscopy enable high-throughput whole-mouse-brain mapping of immediate early gene expression such as c-Fos."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) Whole-brain immediate-early-gene mapping has been applied to characterize individual compounds including psilocybin and ketamine."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) If whole-brain IEG mapping is to function as a screening platform rather than only characterize individual exemplars, its feasibility and accuracy should be evaluated across a larger and more diverse compound panel."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) Developing whole-brain IEG mapping as a drug screen requires evaluating its accuracy across more compounds than individual exemplar studies."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) The study measures brain-wide c-Fos expression across multiple conditions that include a variety of psychedelics and a vehicle control."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) Measuring multiple conditions and using validated interpretable classification can address the need to evaluate feasibility and accuracy for a screening-oriented larger-panel design."];
  "R24" [label="(0,0,0) Classification performance is evaluated using both one-versus-rest and one-versus-one tasks."];
  "R25" [label="(0,0,0) Shapley additive explanations are implemented to interpret the machine-learning models."];
  "R26" [label="(0,0,0) Brain-wide imaging is considered promising for preclinical drug discovery."];
  "R27" [label="(0,0,0) There is growing interest in synthesizing novel psychedelic-inspired analogs with improved pharmacokinetics while retaining therapeutic effects."];
  "R28" [label="(0,0,0) Immediate-early-gene and c-Fos measurements provide a drug-responsive, plasticity-linked, high-throughput whole-brain readout that can motivate screening applications."];
  "R29" [label="(0,0,0) Classification evaluation together with SHAP-based model interpretation provides key components for an interpretable machine-learning classification approach."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Ketamine and esketamine have therapeutic relevance for depression, with ketamine studied for antidepressant efficacy and esketamine approved for treatment-resistant depression."];
  "R30" [label="(0,0,0) When a complementary screening need, a brain-wide c-Fos readout, larger-panel validation, and interpretable classification components are present, it is reasonable to investigate brain-wide c-Fos imaging plus explainable machine learning as a preclinical screening approach."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Some interventions involving MDMA or esketamine are being clinically tested or used for neuropsychiatric disorders such as PTSD or treatment-resistant depression."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Desirable properties for novel psychedelic-inspired analogs include minimizing perceptual effects and eliminating cardiovascular risks."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Growing interest in optimizing novel psychedelic-inspired analogs motivates scalable screening methods to prioritize candidates with desired therapeutic and safety-related profiles."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Molecular screening approaches for candidate compounds can include in silico docking to 5-HT2A receptor structure and measurement of downstream G-protein or beta-arrestin pathway activation."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Behavioral screening can test candidate compounds in animals for defined phenotypes, and automation of simple movement-pattern characterization can increase throughput."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Existing target-based molecular and complex behavioral screening approaches have limitations: one assumes 5-HT2A as a key therapeutic mediator, and the other can have weak predictive power for human drug efficacy."];
  "c_plasticity_screen" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given that psychedelics and ketamine enhance plasticity and the background idea that shared plasticity suggests a screening readout, therefore screening could focus on quantifying neural plasticity indicators in native brain tissue."];
  "k_limits_imply_complement" [label="(0,0,0) If existing screening approaches rely on unproven mechanistic assumptions and/or have weak human predictiveness, then adding a complementary screening modality is warranted."];
  "k_need_screen" [label="(0,0,0) If a field aims to optimize and downselect among thousands of new analogs, then it needs scalable screening methods to prioritize candidates."];
  "k_plasticity_screen_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If multiple therapeutically relevant compounds share plasticity-enhancing effects, then quantifying plasticity indicators could serve as a mechanistically grounded screening readout."];
  "k_scaleup_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If a method is to function as a screening platform, then it must be validated for feasibility/accuracy across a larger, diverse panel rather than only 1–2 exemplars."];
  "k_validation_integrate" [label="(0,0,0) If feasibility/accuracy must be shown on a larger panel, then measuring multiple drug conditions and using validated interpretable classification directly addresses that requirement."];
  "n10_s9_v4" [label="(9,4,0) Currently, an ideal goal for novel analogs is eliminating cardiovascular risks."];
  "n15_s12_v1" [label="(12,1,0) Currently, molecular screening can dock candidate compounds to the 5-HT2A receptor structure (in silico)."];
  "n16_s12_v3" [label="(12,3,0) Currently, molecular screening measures activation of downstream G-protein and beta-arrestin pathways."];
  "n17_s13_v2" [label="(13,2,0) Currently, the target-based molecular approach assumes 5-HT2A is the key mediator of therapeutic effect."];
  "n19_s14_v2" [label="(14,2,0) Currently, behavioral screening tests candidate compounds in animals for defined phenotypes."];
  "n20_s15_v2" [label="(15,2,0) Currently, automation of simple movement-pattern characterizations can increase throughput."];
  "n21_s16_v3" [label="(16,3,0) Currently, complex depression-relevant behavioral assays have weak predictive power for drug efficacy in humans."];
  "n23_s18_v1" [label="(18,1,0) Currently, classic psychedelics and ketamine share the ability to enhance neural plasticity in the brain."];
  "n24_s18_v2" [label="(18,2,0) Currently, neural plasticity enhancement is evidenced by rapid dendritic spine growth after a single ketamine dose (rodent medial frontal cortex)."];
  "n25_s18_v4" [label="(18,4,0) Currently, dendritic spine growth has been observed after a single psilocybin dose."];
  "n26_s19_v1" [label="(19,1,0) Currently, a promising approach may focus on quantifying indicators of neural plasticity in native brain tissues."];
  "n27_s20_v1" [label="(20,1,0) Currently, immediate early genes (IEGs) are activated in response to increased firing activity."];
  "n31_s22_v2" [label="(22,2,0) Currently, classic psychedelic administration induces increases in expression of c-Fos (an IEG)."];
  "n33_s23_v2" [label="(23,2,0) Currently, light sheet fluorescence microscopy advances enable high-throughput whole-mouse-brain mapping of IEG expression such as c-Fos."];
  "n35_s24_v1" [label="(24,1,0) Currently, whole-brain IEG mapping has been applied to characterize the impact of psilocybin."];
  "n36_s24_v2" [label="(24,2,0) Currently, whole-brain IEG mapping has been applied to characterize the impact of ketamine."];
  "n39_s27_v3" [label="(27,3,0) Currently, the study measures brain-wide c-Fos expression across 8 drug conditions including a variety of psychedelics."];
  "n3_s4_v2" [label="(4,2,0) Currently, subanesthetic ketamine has been studied for efficacy in treating depression."];
  "n41_s27_v5" [label="(27,5,0) Currently, the study includes a vehicle control among the 8 conditions."];
  "n43_s28_v2" [label="(28,2,0) Currently, performance is evaluated in one-versus-rest classification tasks."];
  "n44_s28_v3" [label="(28,3,0) Currently, performance is evaluated in one-versus-one classification tasks."];
  "n45_s29_v1" [label="(29,1,0) Currently, Shapley additive explanations (SHAP) are implemented to interpret the ML models."];
  "n47_s30_v2" [label="(30,2,0) Currently, brain-wide imaging is considered promising for preclinical drug discovery."];
  "n4_s5_v2" [label="(5,2,0) Currently, the FDA approved esketamine nasal spray for treatment-resistant depression."];
  "n5_s6_v2" [label="(6,2,0) Currently, MDMA-assisted psychotherapy is being tested for moderate to severe PTSD in phase III trials."];
  "n7_s8_v1" [label="(8,1,0) Currently, there is growing excitement for synthesizing novel psychedelic-inspired analogs."];
  "n8_s9_v2" [label="(9,2,0) Currently, an ideal goal for novel analogs is improved pharmacokinetics while retaining therapeutic effects."];
  "n9_s9_v3" [label="(9,3,0) Currently, an ideal goal for novel analogs is minimizing perceptual effects."];
